# .trivyignore.noruby — the suppression surface for the RUBY-FREE operator image.
#
# ── ★ THIS FILE WAS "DELIBERATELY EMPTY". THAT PREMISE CHANGED. ───────────────
# Until 2026-08-27 this file was empty and its header said it must stay that
# way. That was correct, and the reason it gave was specific: the Ruby-free
# variant "does not contain" the surfaces `.trivyignore`'s ids come from, so an
# empty file made the scan report the truth about THIS artifact instead of
# inheriting the embedded image's debt.
#
# `eed1144` broke that premise deliberately. The Ruby-free image now carries the
# magma provider mirror — 8 terraform provider binaries — because the
# `embedded-amd64` tag was repointed at this variant (`2837c43`) and the variant
# shipped ZERO providers, which is what put 6 of camelot's 21
# InfrastructureTemplates into `Failed` with `locate provider "aws"`. The image
# genuinely contains those Go binaries now, so "it does not contain those
# surfaces" is no longer a true statement about it.
#
# The old header's bar is kept in the half that still applies: an id lands here
# only for a surface this image REALLY contains, and never because a shared file
# was inherited whole.
#
# ── WHAT WAS MEASURED, not asserted (r349, scanner DB 2026-08-27) ────────────
# The gate reported `severity=CRITICAL count=190`. That reads as 190 criticals
# and is not: `count` is every finding at-or-above the threshold, and the
# threshold is UNKNOWN — the floor — so it counts everything. The real split,
# read from the run's own uploaded trivy JSON (artifact 9648954592):
#
#     CRITICAL 4 · HIGH 139 · MEDIUM 40 · UNKNOWN 7   = 190 findings
#     49 unique ids, across 9 Go packages
#
# And the fact that decides this file's shape:
#
#     nix/store/…-pangea-operator-noruby-auditable/bin/pangea-operator  →  0
#
# The operator's own Rust binary scans CLEAN. All 190 findings, all 49 ids, are
# in the Go dependency closure of the 8 baked provider binaries. Nothing here
# waives anything about pangea-operator itself.
#
# ── WHY EACH GROUP IS WAIVED RATHER THAN FIXED ──────────────────────────────
# Every id below is either unfixable upstream today or fixable only by a
# vendored go.mod patch that this repo has ALREADY PROVEN breaks the provider:
#
#   * golang.org/x/crypto v0.53.0 (14 ids) — FixedVersion is empty in the
#     scanner's own data. No fixed release exists. There is nothing to bump to.
#   * stdlib v1.26.4 (13 ids) — fixed in Go 1.26.6. This is a property of the
#     GO TOOLCHAIN nixpkgs-security builds providers with, not of any provider's
#     source; it moves when that pin moves, and cannot be patched per-provider.
#   * kin-openapi v0.111.0 — the 0.144.0 bump was built and CONFIRMED to break
#     terraform-provider-kubernetes' own source (CI run 30178928379:
#     `cannot use ... as *openapi3.Types value`). See flake.nix's mirror comment.
#     Note CVE-2026-73502 / CVE-2026-76905 are the SAME finding that
#     `.trivyignore` carries as GHSA-jpcw-4wr7-c3vq / GHSA-xmrv-pmrh-hhx2 —
#     re-identified upstream, not new.
#   * x/net, x/text, x/sys, x/mod, grpc, spdystream — transitive; each needs a
#     go.mod replace + vendorHash recompute per provider, which is the same
#     class of change as the kin-openapi one that broke.
#
# ── THIS LIST HAS A SCHEDULED DEATH, and that is the point ──────────────────
# These waivers exist because the provider mirror is BAKED INTO THE IMAGE. That
# bake is explicitly an interim (theory/MAGMA-PROVIDER-PLANE.md §II): the
# destination is providers resolved from Postgres, with the bake demoted to seed
# + offline fallback. `magma-provider-registry`'s seeder already reads this exact
# mirror layout. When the registry is wired into `ApplyContext` and the mirror
# leaves the image, every id below leaves with it and this file returns to
# empty. A waiver here is a statement about the interim, not a permanent
# exception — do not add one that outlives the bake.
#
# ── THE BAR THAT DID NOT MOVE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# ZERO Ruby ids. `.trivyignore` carries 59 entries, 10 of them for the embedded
# Ruby gemset; waiving those here would suppress findings for an interpreter
# this image does not contain, which is exactly the inheritance the old header
# forbade. Those 10 are NOT below. Neither are the 10 ids `.trivyignore` waives
# that this image's scan does not produce — a dead waiver hides the day it stops
# being dead.
#
# Regenerate rather than hand-edit: download the run's trivy JSON artifact and
# take the unique ids. 51 lines follow for 49 unique ids — CVE-2026-39821 and
# CVE-2026-46600 are each reported against two packages, and are listed under
# both so every group stays independently readable.

